Application for use of any city park or city building by any individual, organization or group of individuals, for whatever purpose shall be in writing on a form prescribed by the city, which form shall provide spaces for the following information to be inserted:
(1) The correct name of the person, firm, corporation, group or organization making the application;
(2) Street and post office address of such applicant;
(3) Purpose for which such city park, building or property is desired;
(4) When such use is desired of such city park, building or property;
(5) The estimated number of persons to be in attendance or present on the occasion such park, building or property is desired;
(6) Full and complete statement of purposes and objects for which such building, park or other property is to be used;
(7) If the purpose for which the use is desired is for a meeting of some kind, full, complete and truthful statement of purposes of such meeting shall be set forth.
(Code 1959, § 15-3)

1355:
(a) Purpose. The plan and program are enacted for the following purposes:
(1) To enhance the quality of life in the city;
(2) To enhance the built environment in the city;
(3) To revitalize the city's neighborhoods;
(4) To expose the city's residents and visitors to high quality art in public spaces;
(5) To prevent the destruction of the natural beauty and environment of the City;
(6) To improve the image of the city, regionally and nationally;
(7) To provide opportunities for local and regional artists; and
(8) To develop policies, guidelines and procedures for the management of the city's public art program.
(b) Findings.
(1) Over the past forty (40) years, more than seven hundred (700) U.S. cities and counties have developed percent for art programs to provide aesthetic enhancement of the built environment.
(2) As part of the development of the city's public art master plan, residents of East Point were surveyed to determine their views on public art.
(3) Public art can promote a positive image of the city and can instill pride by the residents of East Point.
(4) Public art can promote economic development and improve property values.
(5) Public art can improve the aesthetic environment and the quality of experience of the built environment, enhancing the quality of life for residents and visitors.
(6) Public art can mitigate the undesired sense of uniformity and loss of human scale and orientation.
(7) Public art in private development will mitigate the impacts of private development on East Point's neighborhoods and communities.
(8) A public art commission would be the best structure to advise on the public art program and to advise the city council on matters relating to public art.
(Ord. No. 108-019, § 1, 6-17-19)
